+// --- awp: block, shade and box-drawing glyphs as geometry -------------------
+//
+// Ghostty rasterises U+2500-U+259F itself rather than taking them from the
+// font, which is why a progress bar looks like a progress bar there and like
+// moire here. Three failures come from drawing them as text:
+//
+//   - shade characters are literal dot patterns in the font outline. Tiled at
+//     exact cell boundaries they read as flat grey; drawn at fractional origins
+//     each cell's dots fall out of phase with its neighbour's, which is the
+//     hatching seen across a progress bar;
+//   - box junctions do not meet, because a glyph's ink is sized to the font's
+//     idea of a cell rather than to this renderer's;
+//   - and anything wider than the measured cell is condensed to fit, which
+//     distorts the pattern further.
+//
+// Rectangles snapped to the cell fix all three: a full block is the cell, a half
+// block is half of it, a shade is a solid fill at a fraction of alpha, and a box
+// line runs from the cell edge to its centre so neighbours meet. Nothing is
+// measured, so nothing can be out of phase.
+//
+// Returns true when it has drawn; the caller falls back to fillText otherwise,
+// so an unhandled codepoint still renders.
